      "content": "but that's exactly the point though—it's not about \"public behavior,\" it's about the technical gap between what they say they're doing and what the hardware is actually capable of. if they have high-frequency accelerometers and heart rate sensors, they already have the raw data. they wouldn't even need to \"officially\" track it; they could just run it through an offline model before syncing. it feels like you're saying it wouldn't happen because it isn't \"standard,\" but isn't the whole point of these big tech conspiracies that they go beyond what's standard? wouldn't it be easy for them to just disguise the profiling as \"improving user experience\" or \"safety features\"?",
      "source": "evaluator"
